        "(lecture03:homework:problem1)=\n",
        "## Problem 1 - Playing with matrices\n",
        "\n",
        "Write Python code that constructs the 90-degree rotation matrix in 2D:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A = \\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "    \\cos(\\pi/2) &  -\\sin(\\pi/2) \\\\\n",
        "    \\sin(\\pi/2) & \\cos(\\pi/2)\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "$$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "oSq0NucDQk7A",
        "outputId": "535865b1-77de-48de-ae40-3f7456a7716b"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "array([[ 0, -1],\n",
              "       [ 1,  0]])"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 19
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# your code here\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import math\n",
        "#A=np.zeros((2,2))\n",
        "#A[0]=[round(math.cos(math.pi/2),7), round(-math.sin(math.pi/2),7)]\n",
        "A=np.array([[0,-1],[1,0]])\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "GnA3_FCAQk7A"
      },
      "source": [
        "Verify that the matrix $A$ rotates the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}$ by 90 degrees counterclockwise. That is, $A x = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "yN1H31osQk7B",
        "outputId": "97ba147f-dccc-45c3-b488-b6122b971d32"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "array([[0],\n",
              "       [1]])"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 23
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# your code here\n",
        "x=np.array([[1],[0]])\n",
        "ax = A.dot(x)\n",
        "ax"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4NbW51RjQk7B"
      },
      "source": [
        "Create the matrix:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "B = A\\cdot A\n",
        "$$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "Zu9bHBDcQk7C",
        "outputId": "98eb866a-31b5-4e47-9668-f742e132f853"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "[[-1  0]\n",
            " [ 0 -1]]\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# your code here\n",
        "#.dot acts as matrix multiplicaiton between two matrices in python (same function as A@A).\n",
        "#I'm assuming this isn't asking for a element-wise multiplicaiton of course.\n",
        "B=A.dot(A)\n",
        "print(B)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4HK7RdeLQk7C"
      },
      "source": [
        "Is $B$ a rotation matrix? What is the effect of $B$ on the vector $x$? Hint: To verify that $B$ is a rotation matrix, you need to check that $B^T\\cdot B = I$ (i.e., that $B$ is orthogonal) and that the determinant of $B$ is 1. You can use the ``np.linalg.det()`` function to compute the determinant of a matrix."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "VFxd4a2dQk7D",
        "outputId": "33c9535e-07d4-4e44-851c-908544c7cd84"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "[[-1]\n",
            " [ 0]]\n",
            "[[1 0]\n",
            " [0 1]]\n",
            "1.0\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# your code here\n",
        "bx = B.dot(x)\n",
        "print(bx)#yes a rotation, x was just negated which makes sense because B is the negative unit matrix, it's a 180 degree reflection.\n",
        "print(B.T@B)# yes this equals I\n",
        "print(np.linalg.det(B)) #yes det(B)=1"
      ]
    },
